\begin{enumerate}

\item
\textbf{(a)} Factor
\[
2^{15}-1=32,767.
\]

Since $15=3\cdot5$,
\[
2^{15}-1=(2^5)^3-1.
\]

Using the difference of cubes,
\[
(2^5)^3-1^3
=(2^5-1)(2^{10}+2^5+1).
\]

Therefore,
\[
2^{15}-1=31(1024+32+1)
=31(1057).
\]

Since
\[
1057=7\cdot151,
\]
the complete factorization is
\[
\boxed{32,767=7\cdot31\cdot151}.
\]

\textbf{(b)} We need an integer $x$ such that
\[
1<x<2^{32767}-1
\]
and $x$ divides $2^{32767}-1$.

Since
\[
7\mid32767,
\]
we know that
\[
2^7-1\mid2^{32767}-1.
\]

Because
\[
2^7-1=127,
\]
we can choose
\[
\boxed{x=127}.
\]


\item
Consider the values of $3^n-1$ and $3^n-2^n$:

\[
\begin{array}{c|c|c|c|c}
n & 3^n-1 & \text{Prime?} & 3^n-2^n & \text{Prime?}\\
\hline
1 & 2 & \text{Yes} & 1 & \text{No}\\
2 & 8 & \text{No} & 5 & \text{Yes}\\
3 & 26 & \text{No} & 19 & \text{Yes}\\
4 & 80 & \text{No} & 65 & \text{No}\\
5 & 242 & \text{No} & 211 & \text{Yes}\\
6 & 728 & \text{No} & 665 & \text{No}
\end{array}
\]

For $n>1$, $3^n-1$ is even and greater than $2$, so it cannot be prime.

Thus,
\[
\boxed{3^n-1\text{ is prime only when }n=1.}
\]

For $3^n-2^n$, if $n$ is even, then
\[
3^n-2^n
=
\left(3^{n/2}-2^{n/2}\right)
\left(3^{n/2}+2^{n/2}\right),
\]
so it is composite.

Thus, a conjecture is
\[
\boxed{3^n-2^n\text{ can only be prime when }n\text{ is odd}.}
\]


\item
The method is to multiply all of the listed primes together and add $1$.

\textbf{(a)} For $2,3,5,7$:
\[
2\cdot3\cdot5\cdot7+1=211.
\]

Since $211$ is prime,
\[
\boxed{211}
\]
is a prime different from $2,3,5,$ and $7$.

\textbf{(b)} For $2,5,11$:
\[
2\cdot5\cdot11+1=111.
\]

Since
\[
111=3\cdot37,
\]
we can use the prime factor $37$.

Therefore,
\[
\boxed{37}
\]
is a prime different from $2,5,$ and $11$.


\item
Five consecutive integers that are not prime are
\[
\boxed{24,25,26,27,28}.
\]

Indeed,
\[
24=2\cdot12,
\qquad
25=5\cdot5,
\qquad
26=2\cdot13,
\]
\[
27=3\cdot9,
\qquad
28=4\cdot7.
\]

Therefore, all five numbers are composite.


