\item
Suppose there were three primes of the form
\[
p,\qquad p+2,\qquad p+4.
\]

Among any three consecutive odd numbers separated by $2$, one must be divisible by $3$.

The only way that number can still be prime is if it is $3$ itself.

Therefore, the only possible triple is
\[
\boxed{3,5,7}.
\]

Hence,
\[
\boxed{\text{there are no other such prime triplets}.}
\]



\item
We show that $(220,284)$ is amicable.

The positive divisors of $220$ that are smaller than $220$ are
\[
1,2,4,5,10,11,20,22,44,55,110.
\]

Their sum is
\[
1+2+4+5+10+11+20+22+44+55+110
=284.
\]

The positive divisors of $284$ that are smaller than $284$ are
\[
1,2,4,71,142.
\]

Their sum is
\[
1+2+4+71+142
=220.
\]

Thus, the sum of the proper divisors of $220$ is $284$,
and the sum of the proper divisors of $284$ is $220$.

Therefore,
\[
\boxed{(220,284)\text{ is an amicable pair}.}
\]

\end{enumerate}

